1. Create the flow
  Design studio > Set up > Cloud flows > + Create new flow  (or in a solution)
  Trigger: Power Pages > When Power Pages calls a flow
    + Add an input: Text "postcode", Number "orderTotal", Text "contactId"
  Actions: for example HTTP to the delivery API (key from an environment variable or Key Vault),
           Select to shape the slots.
  Final action: Power Pages > Return value(s) to Power Pages
    + Add an output: Text "slots" = string(body('Select'))      Text "status" = ok
  Inputs and outputs are Text, Number or Boolean. Return arrays as a JSON string.

2. Add it to the site and restrict it
  Set up > Cloud flows > + Add cloud flow > pick the flow > Roles: Authenticated Users (or a narrower role) > Add
  Copy the URL shown, of the form:  /_api/cloudflow/v1.0/trigger/<flow guid>
  Only visitors in the selected web roles can call it. Others receive 403.

3. Call it from the page
  // requires the shell token helper present on Power Pages
  function callFlow(url, payload) {
    return shell.ajaxSafePost({
      type: "POST",
      url: url,
      contentType: "application/json",
      data: JSON.stringify({ eventData: JSON.stringify(payload) })
    });
  }

  $("#btnSlots").on("click", function () {
    var $btn = $(this).prop("disabled", true).text("Checking");
    callFlow("/_api/cloudflow/v1.0/trigger/1a2b3c4d-0000-0000-0000-000000000000", {
      postcode: $("#postcode").val(),
      orderTotal: Number($("#total").val())
    }).done(function (response) {
      var result = JSON.parse(response);                 // keys are the output names in lower case
      var slots = JSON.parse(result["slots"] || "[]");
      $("#slots").empty();
      slots.forEach(function (s) { $("#slots").append($("<li>").text(s.label)); });
    }).fail(function (xhr) {
      $("#slotError").text("Slots are unavailable right now. Try again shortly.");
      console.error(xhr.status, xhr.responseText);
    }).always(function () { $btn.prop("disabled", false).text("Check delivery slots"); });
  });
  The body must be { "eventData": "<JSON string of your inputs>" }. Input names match the trigger's inputs.

4. Identify the caller inside the flow
  Do not trust a contact ID sent from the browser. Pass it only as a hint and verify it, or have the page
  send nothing and let the flow act on data the site has already secured. Where the trigger exposes the
  caller's contact, use that value. Check any record ID received against the contact before returning data:
    Dataverse > List rows: Orders, Filter rows: cr1a2_orderid eq <id> and _cr1a2_contact_value eq <verified contact id>

5. Connections and licensing
  The flow runs with the connections of its owner, not the visitor. Keep it in a solution, owned by a
  service account, with connection references.
  It is covered by the site's licence when in the same environment and registered with the site.

6. Limits
  The call is synchronous: the page waits for Return value(s). Keep the flow under about 120 seconds.
  Payload size is limited (keep requests and responses small, well under 1 MB).
  Throttling applies per user. Disable the button while a call is running.

7. Asynchronous alternative for long work
  Page: create a "Job" row through the Web API with status Queued.
  Flow: Dataverse trigger on the Job row > do the work > update status and result.
  Page: poll the Job row every few seconds, or show the result on the next visit.

8. Deployment
  Flow and site travel in the same solution. After import, confirm the flow is on and still linked under
  Set up > Cloud flows, and that the role assignment is present in the target.

1. Create the script
  Excel for the web > Automate > New Script. Scripts are saved to your OneDrive (Documents/Office Scripts)
  or can be saved to a SharePoint library for team use.

Script: write rows to a table in one operation
function main(workbook: ExcelScript.Workbook, rowsJson: string, clearFirst: boolean = false): string {
  const rows: (string | number | boolean)[][] = JSON.parse(rowsJson);
  const sheet = workbook.getWorksheet("Data");
  let table = sheet.getTable("tblOrders");
  if (!table) { throw new Error("Table tblOrders not found"); }
  if (clearFirst && table.getRowCount() > 0) {
    table.getRangeBetweenHeaderAndTotal().delete(ExcelScript.DeleteShiftDirection.up);
  }
  if (rows.length > 0) { table.addRows(-1, rows); }
  sheet.getRange("H1").setValue(new Date().toISOString());
  return JSON.stringify({ added: rows.length, total: table.getRowCount() });
}

2. Build the input in the flow
  Select
    From: body('Get_items')?['value']
    Map (switch to text mode to output an array per item):
      @createArray(item()?['OrderNumber'], item()?['Customer'], formatDateTime(item()?['OrderDate'],'yyyy-MM-dd'), item()?['Amount'], item()?['Status/Value'])
  The order of values must match the table's columns.

3. Run it
  Excel Online (Business) > Run script
    Location / Document Library / File: the workbook
    Script: Write orders
    rowsJson:   @{string(body('Select'))}
    clearFirst: true
  Result: outputs('Run_script')?['body/result']            parse with json() if you returned JSON
  Script stored in SharePoint: use "Run script from SharePoint library" and pick the .osts file.

Script: read a range that is not a table and return objects
interface Row { id: string; name: string; amount: number; }
function main(workbook: ExcelScript.Workbook, sheetName: string): Row[] {
  const values = workbook.getWorksheet(sheetName).getUsedRange().getValues();
  const out: Row[] = [];
  for (let i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {              // skip the header row
    if (values[i][0] === "") continue;
    out.push({ id: String(values[i][0]), name: String(values[i][1]), amount: Number(values[i][2]) });
  }
  return out;
}
  The flow receives an array of objects: body('Run_script')?['result'], usable in Select or Apply to each.

Script: update rows by key
function main(workbook: ExcelScript.Workbook, updatesJson: string): number {
  const updates: { id: string; status: string }[] = JSON.parse(updatesJson);
  const table = workbook.getTable("tblOrders");
  const range = table.getRangeBetweenHeaderAndTotal();
  const values = range.getValues();
  const idCol = table.getColumnByName("OrderNumber").getIndex();
  const statusCol = table.getColumnByName("Status").getIndex();
  const map = new Map(updates.map(u => [u.id, u.status]));
  let changed = 0;
  for (const row of values) {
    const s = map.get(String(row[idCol]));
    if (s !== undefined && row[statusCol] !== s) { row[statusCol] = s; changed++; }
  }
  range.setValues(values);
  return changed;
}

Rules and limits
  Read and write whole ranges (getValues / setValues) instead of cell by cell.
  Run script: 120 second timeout when called from a flow, request and response size about 5 MB,
  and roughly 1,600 calls per day per user.
  Split very large inputs into chunks of a few thousand rows.
  No external calls from a script run by a flow (fetch is blocked). Pass data in as parameters.
  Parameters: string, number, boolean, arrays and simple objects. Default values make them optional.
  The script runs as the flow connection's user, who needs edit access to the workbook and the script.
  Errors thrown in the script fail the action with the message, so throw clear errors.

Sharing
  For production: save scripts to a SharePoint library, use the SharePoint variant of the action and a
  service account connection, so the flow does not depend on one person's OneDrive.

Enable
  Settings > Updates > New (or Preview) > User-defined functions: On
  For functions that change data: also enable User-defined types and behaviour UDFs where listed.

Where: App object > Formulas property

Syntax
  FunctionName(Param1: Type, Param2: Type): ReturnType = expression;
  Types: Text, Number, Decimal, Boolean, Date, DateTime, Color, GUID, Hyperlink, plus user-defined types.

Pure functions (no side effects)
  FullName(first: Text, last: Text): Text = Trim(first & " " & last);

  Initials(name: Text): Text =
      Upper(Left(name, 1) & Mid(name, Find(" ", name & " ") + 1, 1));

  DaysOverdue(due: Date): Number = Max(0, DateDiff(due, Today(), TimeUnit.Days));

  StatusColor(status: Text): Color =
      Switch(status, "Approved", ColorValue("#107C10"), "Rejected", ColorValue("#D13438"),
                     "Pending", ColorValue("#986F0B"), ColorValue("#616161"));

  CanEditRequest(status: Text, ownerEmail: Text): Boolean =
      (status = "Draft" && Lower(ownerEmail) = Lower(User().Email)) || nfIsAdmin;

  FormatMoney(amount: Number): Text = Text(amount, "$#,##0.00");

Use them
  lblName.Text:          FullName(ThisItem.FirstName, ThisItem.LastName)
  cirAvatar text:        Initials(ThisItem.'Assigned To'.DisplayName)
  lblStatus.Color:       StatusColor(ThisItem.Status.Value)
  btnEdit.DisplayMode:   If(CanEditRequest(ThisItem.Status.Value, ThisItem.Author.Email), DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)
  Gallery sort:          SortByColumns(AddColumns(Requests, Overdue, DaysOverdue(DueDate)), "Overdue", SortOrder.Descending)
  Functions can call other functions and named formulas (nfIsAdmin above).

Record and table parameters with user-defined types
  RequestType := Type({ ID: Number, Title: Text, Amount: Number, Status: Text });
  RequestsType := Type([RequestType]);

  IsHighValue(r: RequestType): Boolean = r.Amount > 5000 && r.Status <> "Cancelled";
  TotalAmount(rows: RequestsType): Number = Sum(rows, Amount);

  Call: IsHighValue({ ID: ThisItem.ID, Title: ThisItem.Title, Amount: ThisItem.Amount, Status: ThisItem.Status.Value })
  Extra columns on the passed record are ignored. Missing ones are blank.

Behaviour functions (side effects), body in curly braces
  LogAction(action: Text, recordId: Number): Void = {
      Patch(AuditLog, Defaults(AuditLog), { Title: action, RecordId: recordId, User: User().Email, When: Now() })
  };

  SetStatus(recordId: Number, newStatus: Text): Boolean = {
      IfError(
          Patch(Requests, LookUp(Requests, ID = recordId), { Status: { Value: newStatus } });
          LogAction("Status: " & newStatus, recordId);
          true,
          Notify(FirstError.Message, NotificationType.Error); false
      )
  };

  btnApprove.OnSelect:  If(SetStatus(gblSelected.ID, "Approved"), Navigate(scrHome))
  Behaviour functions can only be called from behaviour properties (OnSelect and similar).

Rules and limits
  Declared once, available on every screen. Order of declaration does not matter.
  Pure functions cannot call Set, Patch, Navigate, Notify or Collect. Use the brace form for those.
  Parameters are required: there are no optional parameters or overloads. Pass Blank() where needed.
  Recursion is not supported.
  Delegation: calling a function inside Filter on a data source makes that part non-delegable. Apply
  functions to results, or keep delegable conditions inline.
  Names must not clash with built-in functions, controls or data sources.

Naming
  PascalCase verbs or questions: CanEditRequest, FormatMoney, DaysOverdue. Keep a short comment above each:
  // True when the signed-in user may edit the request

Compared with other reuse options
  Named formula: a value with no parameters.  UDF: logic with parameters.
  Component with custom functions: reuse across apps through a component library.
  Hidden button + Select(): the old workaround. Replace with behaviour UDFs.

